[description]

Bitbucket [b]pull requests[/b] parallel other forges: diff, comments, tasks, and required builds before merge. [b]Branch permissions[/b] can restrict who pushes directly, who can merge, and whether fast-forward is required—combine with pipeline gates to prevent accidental history rewrites.

[b]Fork vs branch workflows:[/b] public OSS often uses forks; internal orgs may use branch-only flows with strict default branch protection. Fork PRs still end as Git merges on the target.

[b]Build status integration:[/b] pipelines publish build badges consumed by PRs; third-party CI can integrate via commit statuses if configured.

[b]Large diffs:[/b] performance settings and shallow clones help reviewers; etiquette (smaller MRs) still dominates perceived velocity.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] read Bitbucket’s merge checks as code+policy: Git proves the graph, Bitbucket enforces who may move protected refs.
